---
title: Export Images
sidebar:
  order: 14
---

Use the media export when you need the photographs themselves rather than the
records that describe them. It is the quickest way to pull a set of field
images out of a project for a grant report, a permit report, a presentation, or
a colleague who does not use NAHPU.

There are two ways to export. Export one file when you want full control over
its size and quality, or export a selection as a single archive when you need
many files at once. Audio and video are included in a selection, but the image
options below only apply to images.

## Export a single file

Open the media item, then choose `Export`.

{% steps %}

1. Check the file name. NAHPU proposes the current name of the file, and you
   can turn on `Append date` to add today's date.

2. Choose the format. `Original` always works. `JPEG`, `PNG`, and `WebP` are
   offered when NAHPU can read the image, which covers `.jpg`, `.jpeg`,
   `.png`, and `.webp` sources.

3. Turn on `Resize image` if the file is larger than you need. The aspect ratio
   is locked, so typing a width updates the height, and neither value can go
   above the original dimensions shown above the switch.

4. Adjust `JPEG quality` when exporting to JPEG. The default of 85 percent is a
   good balance of size and detail. Lower it for email or upload over a slow
   connection.

5. Select a destination folder, then `Export`. When the file is written, the
   button becomes `Share`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="note" %}

Formats that NAHPU cannot decode, such as RAW camera files, can still be
exported in their original format. The dialog says so instead of offering the
conversion options.

{% /aside %}

## Export a selection as an archive

Open the media gallery from any record media panel, or use the media panel of
the record itself. Turn on selection, choose the files you want, then select
`Export`. This opens the `Export selected media` screen.

The summary lists how many files were selected, how many were found on disk,
how many are images, audio, video, or other, and the total source size. Files
that cannot be found are reported as warnings rather than silently dropped.

The settings panel has:

- **Archive format**. `TAR.GZ` produces a smaller file and is the better choice
  for upload. macOS, Linux, and Windows 11 open it with no extra software.
  Choose `ZIP` when the archive is going to an older Windows machine, because
  Windows 10 and earlier need a separate tool to open `.tar.gz`.
- **Image format**. `Original` copies the files unchanged. `JPEG`, `PNG`, or
  `WebP` converts every readable image in the selection.
- **Limit image dimensions**. Available once you convert. Enter a maximum
  width or height in pixels, and NAHPU constrains whichever side is longer.
  Images already smaller than the limit are not upscaled.
- **JPEG quality**. Applies when the image format is JPEG.
- **File name**, **Append date**, and the destination folder.

Inside the archive, files are grouped into `specimen`, `site`, `event`,
`narrative`, and `other` folders so you can tell where each file came from.
Audio and video are copied unchanged regardless of the image format.

{% aside type="caution" %}

A large selection can take several minutes, especially when converting. The
screen shows progress and a `Cancel` button while it runs. Keep NAHPU open
until it finishes, and check the exported and skipped counts and any warnings
in the summary before you send the archive on.

{% /aside %}

## Choosing settings

- For a report or a slide deck, convert to `JPEG` and limit the longer side to
  around 2000 px. This usually reduces an archive by an order of magnitude with
  no visible loss.
- For an upload over a field internet connection, use `TAR.GZ`, `JPEG`, and a
  lower quality value.
- For archiving or for a collaborator who will process the images, keep
  `Original` so nothing is recompressed.

